<ArticleLayout slug="the-extruder-an-unmatched-tool-for-livestock-production">
  <p>
    New plant productions can be used in ways that improve their
    digestibility and eliminate toxic or antinutritional factors before they
    are incorporated into a formula. The almost instantaneous heat treatment
    of the HT-ST type (High Temperature-Short Time) specific to extrusion
    allows storage without deterioration for several months.
  </p>
  <p>
    While the first applications of extrusion in animal feed targeted pet food
    and aquaculture, extruders gradually appeared on the market that were
    more advantageous economically, thanks to their modular, robust and
    flexible design.
  </p>
  <p>
    These extruders were used specifically for the heat treatment of oilseeds,
    as Dr Legoy stated at the time:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    “The technology used allows the destruction of contaminating germs, of
    certain antinutritional factors (antitrypsic factors in whole soybeans,
    glucosinolates in rapeseed) or enzymatic factors (lipases and
    lipo-oxidases involved in the oxidation of oils, for example). To this
    must be added the destruction of the cell walls, allowing better
    digestibility of the energy contained in the product (oil, gelatinised
    starches), and the heat treatment of the proteins, allowing protein
    insolubilisation, particularly in feed intended for ruminants.”
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The applications of extrusion in animal feed are broad. The internal
    configuration of the extruder is easily adaptable to allow all kinds of
    operations. A few examples:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the processing of whole soybeans for their incorporation into pig and
      poultry feed at significant percentages,
    </li>
    <li>
      the manufacture of high-performance feed or supplements for
      high-production ruminants,
    </li>
    <li>
      the production of special feed - pet food, shrimp feed, floating feed or
      feed with a controlled sinking rate,
    </li>
    <li>
      the stabilisation of by-products: rice bran, trimming or filleting
      waste, and so on.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
